{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     services/posthog — product analytics, EU-hosted by default.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Renders nothing unless @custom.posthog_key is set. The host comes from
     @custom.posthog_host and defaults to https://eu.i.posthog.com — the EU
     cloud, deliberately. PostHog stores raw, per-person event data (that is
     the product), and WHERE it is stored is the GDPR question: the EU
     cluster keeps a European reader's data inside the EEA, which is the
     posture a site with EU readers should default to. Choosing the US
     cluster afterwards means a new project and losing history, so the
     default has to be right on day one; the US host is one settings edit
     for a publisher who genuinely wants it.

     WHAT CHANGED FROM POSTHOG'S OFFICIAL SNIPPET, AND WHY. The official
     snippet is a minified stub that shims ~60 method names onto a fake
     window.posthog so calls made before the real bundle arrives are queued
     and replayed. This theme makes no such calls — init happens in the
     loader's own onload and nothing else here touches posthog — so the stub
     is dead weight and, worse, unreviewable: a hand-transcribed blob of
     minified code in a template is exactly the kind of thing nobody can
     diff against upstream. What is KEPT from the official snippet is every
     behaviour that matters: the same /static/array.js bundle, fetched from
     the same assets host (the ".i.posthog.com" → "-assets.i.posthog.com"
     rewrite is lifted verbatim, and passes a self-hosted URL through
     untouched), async and crossorigin="anonymous" as upstream sets it, and
     the same init options. `defaults: "2025-05-24"` is PostHog's own
     current recommended defaults set (verified against the shipped bundle)
     — history-API pageviews and identified-only person profiles — and
     pinning the date is the point: PostHog changes behaviour by defaults
     version, not under your feet.

     THE WEIGHT is the reason this file should stay unset until the site
     has product questions to answer: array.js is ~267 KB of JavaScript
     (~85 KB over the wire), measured. abstract/10-user-tracking.md is the
     argument for when that price becomes worth paying.

     CONSENT AND SIGNALS, same contract as ga4.hbs: GPC/DNT wins outright;
     then no gate on the page means load normally, "granted" loads now,
     null waits, "denied" loads nothing. PostHog has its own opt-out and
     cookieless modes, but the strictest and simplest form is the one a
     partial can enforce: no consent, no script, no request.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#if @custom.posthog_key}}
<script>
(function () {
    "use strict";
    if (navigator.globalPrivacyControl === true ||
        navigator.doNotTrack === "1" || window.doNotTrack === "1") { return; }

    function boot() {
        if (window.__nsPosthog) { return; }
        window.__nsPosthog = true;
        var host = "{{@custom.posthog_host}}".replace(/\/+$/, "") ||
            "https://eu.i.posthog.com";
        var s = document.createElement("script");
        s.async = true;
        s.crossOrigin = "anonymous";
        s.src = host.replace(".i.posthog.com", "-assets.i.posthog.com") +
            "/static/array.js";
        s.onload = function () {
            if (window.posthog && window.posthog.init) {
                window.posthog.init("{{@custom.posthog_key}}", {
                    api_host: host,
                    defaults: "2025-05-24"
                });
            }
        };
        document.head.appendChild(s);
    }

    var gate = window.NSConsent;
    if (!gate) { boot(); }
    else if (gate.state === "granted") { boot(); }
    else if (gate.state === null) { gate.whenGranted(boot); }
})();
</script>
{{/if}}
